---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DENOMINATOR, ENROLLMENT_UNIT, K, MIN_ROSTER } from '../lib/scoring/index.js';
import { adjustedRate, rawRate } from '../lib/scoring/index.js';

/**
 * The worked example is computed with the real scoring functions rather than
 * typed in, so it cannot drift away from the code that produces the table.
 */
const mean = 6;
const examples = [
  { name: 'A 30,000-undergraduate university', points: 180, enrollment: 30_000 },
  { name: 'A 6,000-undergraduate university', points: 48, enrollment: 6_000 },
  { name: 'A 1,200-undergraduate college', points: 36, enrollment: 1_200 },
].map((row) => ({
  ...row,
  raw: rawRate(row.points, row.enrollment),
  adjusted: adjustedRate(row.points, row.enrollment, mean, K),
}));
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Standing Works"
  description="The NCBO standings formula in full: points per 1,000 undergraduates, shrunk toward the league mean, with a minimum roster floor — and an honest account of its limits."
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>How standing works</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This page is the whole method. If the table ever disagrees with what is written here, the table
      is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Ranking clubs on total points rewards big schools for being big. A university with 30,000
      undergraduates has ten times the recruiting pool of one with 3,000, and a table topped by the
      largest campuses every year measures enrollment, not effort.
    </p>
    <p>
      The obvious fix is points per student. That is worse, and it is worse in a way that is easy to
      miss: dividing by a small number inflates the result. A 1,200-student college needs a
      twenty-fifth of the points of a 30,000-student university to post the same rate. Small schools
      then win by construction — the mirror image of the problem we started with. Worse, rates
      measured over small populations swing violently. One good week from a twelve-person club moves
      the national table, and the ranking ends up measuring luck.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do instead</h2>
    <p>
      Each club's rate is pulled toward the league average by an amount that depends on how much
      evidence stands behind it. This is standard practice for rate statistics over uneven sample
      sizes, and it is the same idea behind regression to the mean.
    </p>
    <pre class="formula"><code>raw_rate    = points / (enrollment / {ENROLLMENT_UNIT})
league_mean = Σpoints / Σ(enrollment / {ENROLLMENT_UNIT})    // ranked clubs only
adjusted    = (points + K × league_mean) / (enrollment / {ENROLLMENT_UNIT} + K)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Read the adjustment as: every club is treated as though it also carried
      <strong>K = {K}</strong> thousand students' worth of league-average performance. For a
      30,000-undergraduate university that is about 6% of the denominator and changes almost nothing.
      For a 2,000-undergraduate college it is the same size as the denominator, so its rate lands
      halfway between its own result and the league average.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Nobody is penalised for their school's size.</strong> Clubs are damped in proportion to
      the uncertainty their sample size implies — which is a different thing, and it cuts both ways:
      a small club with a genuinely large result still finishes top, because the result is big enough
      to survive the shrinkage.
    </p>

    <h3>A worked example</h3>
    <p>Assume a league mean of {mean}.00 points per {ENROLLMENT_UNIT.toLocaleString('en-US')} undergraduates.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="ex">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Club</th>
            <th scope="col">Points</th>
            <th scope="col">Undergrads</th>
            <th scope="col">Raw rate</th>
            <th scope="col">Adjusted</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            examples.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                <td class="num">{row.points}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.enrollment.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
                <td class="num">{fmt(row.raw)}</td>
                <td class="num strong">{fmt(row.adjusted)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      All three post a raw rate of {fmt(examples[0]!.raw)}. After adjustment the small college sits
      lower, not because it did worse, but because {examples[2]!.enrollment.toLocaleString('en-US')}{' '}
      students is thin evidence for a national ranking. Give it a second strong season and it stays
      high; the shrinkage does not accumulate against it.
    </p>

    <h2>The minimum roster floor</h2>
    <p>
      A club with fewer than <strong>{MIN_ROSTER} verified members</strong> is listed as
      <strong>"Unranked — building roster"</strong>. It appears in the table, with its points, but
      without a position, and it does not contribute to the league mean.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shrinkage cannot rescue a sample that small, and a heavily-shrunk number still looks like a
      ranking to anyone reading quickly. A visible "not enough data yet" is more honest than a
      position nobody should trust.
    </p>

    <h2>The denominator</h2>
    <p>
      The denominator is <strong>undergraduate enrollment</strong> (<code>{DENOMINATOR}</code>), taken
      from IPEDS, the US Department of Education's public institutional data set.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is a judgment call, and it has losers.</strong> Undergraduate enrollment was chosen
      because NCBO membership is overwhelmingly undergraduate, and using total enrollment would
      penalise universities with large graduate and professional populations for students who were
      never plausible members. But it treats a large part-time or online undergraduate population as
      though it were a recruiting pool on the same terms as a residential one, which it is not. A
      commuter campus is measured against a denominator that overstates its realistic reach.
    </p>
    <p>
      We have not found a denominator without a defect like this. The choice is published as a
      configurable constant so that changing it is a visible decision rather than a quiet one.
    </p>

    <h2>What this method cannot do</h2>
    <ul class="limits">
      <li>
        <strong>K = {K} has not been tuned.</strong> The principled way to set it is to fit it against
        real results — pick the value that best predicts the second half of a season from the first —
        and there is no season yet. {K} is a defensible starting point and nothing more. It will
        change once there is data, and when it does, we will say so.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The floor of {MIN_ROSTER} is likewise a starting point</strong>, picked to be reachable
        by a genuinely new club within a semester.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Points themselves are the bigger lever.</strong> No adjustment to the denominator
        fixes a badly-designed points weighting. Event weights are published per event.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rate statistics reward consistency over peaks.</strong> That is a deliberate choice
        about what the table should measure, not a neutral fact about the maths.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rank movement is only shown when there is real history to move against.</strong> A
        column of zeroes implying "held position" in a season where nothing has happened would be a
        small lie, and it is the kind that erodes trust in the rest of the table.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Both numbers, always</h2>
    <p>
      The standings table shows the raw rate next to the adjusted one on every row. Showing only the
      adjusted figure would ask you to trust a calculation you cannot see; showing only the raw one
      would publish the noise. If you disagree with the adjustment, the unadjusted number is right
      there to argue with.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 3rem;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }
  p {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }
  .formula {
    background: var(--scrim);
    color: var(--on-dark);
    padding: 1rem;
    border-radius: var(--r);
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .ex {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 560px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .ex th,
  .ex td {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .ex thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .num {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .caption {
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .limits {
    margin: 1rem 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .limits li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
